```c
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
int counter = 0;

void* increment(void* arg) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
        counter++;               // now protected
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }
    return NULL;
}
```

This prints exactly 2,000,000 every time. The lock/unlock pair around `counter++` makes the whole load-add-store sequence appear atomic to every other thread — either a thread runs the entire critical section, or it hasn't started yet. Note the cost: 2 million lock/unlock pairs now serialize what used to be two threads running fully in parallel, which is why real code locks the smallest region that needs it, not the whole loop.

A **spinlock** makes a waiting thread loop, repeatedly checking if the lock is free, instead of yielding the CPU:

```c
while (atomic_test_and_set(&lock)) {
    // spin — do nothing, just keep checking
}
```

The upside: the moment the lock is released, a spinning thread notices almost instantly — no OS scheduler round-trip. That makes spinlocks a good fit when the expected wait is shorter than the cost of a context switch (roughly 1–10 microseconds), which is exactly the situation inside an OS kernel or a low-level allocator holding a lock for a handful of instructions.

The downside is just as sharp: a spinning thread burns 100% of a CPU core doing no useful work, and on a single-core machine it can spin until its time slice runs out with zero chance the lock-holder gets to run and release it.
